window.P.push({"n":19,"pt":"VII","ct":"Patient-Facing Chatbots and Triage Assistants","t":"Multimodal Rash-Photo Triage Escalation Script","u":"Use this prompt when your team is drafting the escalation logic and disposition wording a patient-facing chatbot should follow for skin-related complaints where a patient can attach a photo alongside their text description.","no":"Use Gemini here because the task genuinely requires reasoning across an attached image and a text description in a single pass, which is Gemini's clearest structural advantage among the three assistants for this kind of content-design work. ChatGPT and Claude can also process an attached image, but Gemini's native multimodal handling is the platform this book treats as strongest for combined text-plus-image clinical content drafting. Keep the same four-part output structure if you later swap in a different visual complaint, since the format, not the specific symptom, is what makes the draft reusable across a chatbot's full escalation library.","v":["You are a clinical content designer building the escalation logic for a hospital's patient-facing symptom-checker chatbot. A patient has described a red, spreading, warm area on their lower leg: 'It started three days ago as a small red spot and now covers most of my shin, it's warm and a little painful, and I have chills today.' No photo is available for this draft, work from the text alone. Draft: three possible triage dispositions ranging from emergency to self-care; the specific verbal features that would move this case toward the more urgent disposition; one plain-language safety-netting sentence for whichever disposition you recommend; and two follow-up questions the bot should ask before finalizing. Do not state a diagnosis; frame everything as triage guidance for a non-clinician reader.","You are a clinical content designer building the escalation logic for a hospital's patient-facing symptom-checker chatbot. A patient has described a red, spreading, warm area on their lower leg with the message: 'It started three days ago as a small red spot and now covers most of my shin, it's warm and a little painful, and I have chills today.' A photo of the area is attached. Using the text and the image together, draft: (1) three possible triage dispositions ranging from emergency to self-care, (2) the specific visual and verbal features that would move this case toward the more urgent disposition, (3) one plain-language safety-netting sentence for whichever disposition you recommend, and (4) two follow-up questions the bot should ask before finalizing. Do not state a diagnosis; frame everything as triage guidance for a non-clinician reader.","You are a clinical content designer building a reusable escalation-logic template for a hospital's patient-facing symptom-checker chatbot's skin-complaint library. Using this case as the first entry, 'It started three days ago as a small red spot and now covers most of my shin, it's warm and a little painful, and I have chills today,' with a photo attached, produce a structured output with exactly these four labeled sections, held in this format so future complaints can reuse it: 'Triage Dispositions' (emergency to self-care), 'Escalation-Triggering Features' (visual and verbal), 'Safety-Netting Sentence,' and 'Bot Follow-Up Questions' (two). Do not state a diagnosis; frame everything as triage guidance for a non-clinician reader."]});
window.P.push({"n":20,"pt":"VII","ct":"Prompting for Patient Education Materials and Communication","t":"Reading-Level Patient Instruction Draft","u":"Use this prompt when converting a clinical note or discharge instruction into a patient-facing handout at a specified reading level, with clinical values held exactly constant during simplification.","no":"Use ChatGPT here because OpenAI has specifically tuned recent GPT-5.x models for making complex clinical information easier for a lay reader to understand, and its self-reported readability estimate tends to be a reasonably calibrated first check before human review. Gemini and Claude can both perform this task competently, but ChatGPT's demonstrated focus on patient-facing plain-language explanation makes it the strongest default starting point for this specific drafting task.","v":["Rewrite the following discharge instructions for a patient reading at a sixth-grade level. Use short sentences, one idea per sentence, second-person direct address, and clear section headers. Preserve every number, dose, medication name, and timing detail exactly as written; simplify only the surrounding language, never the clinical values themselves. End with one short question the patient could use to check their own understanding of the most important instruction. After the rewrite, state your estimated Flesch-Kincaid grade level, then ask me whether this reading level works for the intended patient population before we save this as a template. Instructions: 'Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily with meals. Begin lisinopril 10 mg once daily each morning. Follow up with primary care in two weeks. Call the clinic if you experience dizziness, swelling, or a blood pressure reading above 160/100.'","I am pasting an entire batch of discharge instruction templates used across our clinic below, not just one: [paste all discharge instruction templates here, including: 'Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily with meals. Begin lisinopril 10 mg once daily each morning. Follow up with primary care in two weeks. Call the clinic if you experience dizziness, swelling, or a blood pressure reading above 160/100.']. Rewrite every instruction set for a patient reading at a sixth-grade level, using short sentences, one idea per sentence, second-person direct address, and clear section headers. Preserve every number, dose, medication name, and timing detail exactly as written in each; simplify only surrounding language. End each rewrite with one comprehension-check question and your estimated Flesch-Kincaid grade level.","Rewrite the following discharge instructions for a patient reading at a sixth-grade level, formatted to our institution's fixed patient-handout template. Structure the output with exactly these labeled sections, held precisely: 'Instructions' (short sentences, one idea per sentence, second-person address, clear headers, every number, dose, medication name, and timing preserved exactly as written), 'Check Your Understanding' (one question), and 'Estimated Reading Level' (your Flesch-Kincaid estimate). Simplify only the surrounding language, never the clinical values. Instructions: 'Continue metformin 500 mg twice daily with meals. Begin lisinopril 10 mg once daily each morning. Follow up with primary care in two weeks. Call the clinic if you experience dizziness, swelling, or a blood pressure reading above 160/100.'"]});
window.P.push({"n":21,"pt":"VII","ct":"Administrative AI: Scheduling, Coding, and Prior Authorization","t":"Structured Prior Authorization Support Draft","u":"Use this prompt when drafting the supporting narrative section of a prior authorization request, linking a payer's coverage criteria to specific chart documentation, before a staff member verifies and submits it.","no":"Use Claude here because Anthropic's Claude for Healthcare is purpose-built for exactly this workflow, with reported integration into CMS coverage databases and FHIR-aware tooling for drafting against real chart data, and because the underlying task, a long, formally structured document another party will read and rely on, plays to Claude's demonstrated strength in disciplined, policy-compliant written output. ChatGPT's broader workflow-automation ecosystem is a reasonable alternative for the intake and submission logistics around this task, but the narrative drafting itself is the specific strength this book attributes most directly to Claude.","v":["Draft the supporting narrative for a prior authorization request for a lumbar MRI. The payer's coverage policy requires documentation of at least six weeks of failed conservative treatment and at least one red-flag or progressive neurological finding. The chart documents: eight weeks of physical therapy with no improvement, NSAID use for four weeks, and a new finding of decreased strength in the left foot dorsiflexors on today's exam. Structure the narrative in three labeled sections: conservative treatment history with dates, the qualifying clinical finding, and a one-sentence medical necessity statement. Use direct, formal payer-facing language, cite the specific chart detail for each criterion, and flag in brackets anything unclear. Then draft the intake checklist and submission steps our staff should follow to send this once verified.","Draft the supporting narrative for a prior authorization request for a lumbar MRI. I am pasting the payer's full coverage policy document below rather than a summary, so nothing in its criteria gets missed: [paste the full payer coverage policy document here]. The chart documents: eight weeks of physical therapy with no improvement, NSAID use for four weeks, and a new finding of decreased strength in the left foot dorsiflexors on today's exam. Using the attached policy's actual language, structure the narrative in three labeled sections: conservative treatment history with dates, the qualifying clinical finding, and a one-sentence medical necessity statement matching the policy's own criteria wording. Cite the specific chart detail and the specific policy clause supporting each criterion, and flag in brackets any criterion not clearly satisfied.","Draft the supporting narrative for a prior authorization request for a lumbar MRI. The payer's coverage policy requires documentation of at least six weeks of failed conservative treatment (physical therapy or NSAIDs) and at least one red-flag or progressive neurological finding. The chart documents: eight weeks of physical therapy with no improvement, NSAID use for four weeks, and a new finding of decreased strength in the left foot dorsiflexors on today's exam. Structure the narrative in exactly three clearly labeled sections, held precisely in this order: (1) conservative treatment history with dates, (2) the qualifying clinical finding, (3) a one-sentence medical necessity statement. Use direct, formal language appropriate for a payer reviewer, cite the specific chart detail supporting each criterion, and flag, in brackets, any criterion the chart data does not clearly satisfy so staff can verify before submission."]});
window.P.push({"n":22,"pt":"VIII","ct":"Bias and Health Equity in Clinical AI","t":"Pre-Deployment Subgroup Blind-Spot Check","u":"Helps a clinical informatics reviewer or AI selection committee member turn a vendor's full technical validation report into a structured list of subgroup performance gaps and follow-up questions before a tool is approved for use.","no":"Use Gemini because this task requires ingesting an entire vendor validation report, potentially dozens of pages, in a single pass rather than pre-summarizing it, which plays directly to Gemini's large-context handling of full source documents. ChatGPT can run the same prompt conversationally and will ask clarifying questions if the report is ambiguous, which is useful for a first pass on a shorter document. Claude is the better choice once the committee needs the assistant to draft the actual structured vendor-question memo or governance write-up, rather than perform the extraction pass itself.","v":["You are a clinical informatics reviewer preparing for an AI-tool selection committee meeting. I will paste or summarize a vendor's technical validation report for a predictive or diagnostic AI tool we are considering. Read it and: list every patient subgroup for which the vendor reports a stratified performance metric, and every subgroup it does not; flag any subgroup with a small sample size that could hide an unstable estimate; identify any input variable with documented differential accuracy across populations; and draft five specific questions the committee should ask the vendor. Present this as a numbered list under those four headings, and ask me a clarifying question if the report is ambiguous about any subgroup's definition.","You are a clinical informatics reviewer preparing for an AI-tool selection committee meeting. I am giving you the vendor's full technical validation report for a predictive or diagnostic AI tool we are considering, pasted or attached in its entirety, potentially dozens of pages: [paste or attach the complete vendor validation report here]. Read the entire document, then: (1) list every patient subgroup (race, ethnicity, sex, age band, language, insurance status, care setting) for which the vendor reports a stratified performance metric, and every subgroup it does not; (2) flag any subgroup with a small reported sample size that could hide an unstable estimate; (3) identify any input variable with documented differential accuracy across patient populations; (4) draft five specific questions the committee should ask the vendor before approval. Present your answer as a numbered list under those four headings.","You are a clinical informatics reviewer turning a completed subgroup performance extraction into a formal governance write-up. Using the subgroup findings I provide below, produce a structured memo with exactly these labeled sections, held precisely: 'Subgroups With Reported Stratified Performance,' 'Subgroups Not Disclosed,' 'Small-Sample Subgroups With Potentially Unstable Estimates,' 'Input Variables With Documented Differential Accuracy,' and 'Five Questions for the Vendor Before Approval.' Write it as a document the selection committee can file and reference directly, using hedged, conservative language throughout and never asserting equivalence the underlying data does not support. Findings to structure: [paste subgroup findings here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":23,"pt":"VIII","ct":"Automation Bias and the Human-in-the-Loop Standard","t":"Override Pattern Review Protocol","u":"Helps a clinical quality improvement or informatics lead draft a structured, repeatable protocol for periodically reviewing a specific AI tool's override rate and distinguishing appropriate clinical exceptions from alert fatigue or automation bias, ready to bring to a quality committee.","no":"Use Claude because this is a long, structurally disciplined document that another team, the quality committee, will read and rely on largely as written, exactly the kind of policy-adherent long-form output Claude's healthcare-oriented tooling is built for. ChatGPT is a reasonable second choice if the team wants a more conversational first draft with follow-up questions before finalizing the structure. Do not default to Gemini's long-context strength here; that advantage matters for ingesting an existing large source document, and this task is drafting a new protocol from scratch, not summarizing one.","v":["Act as a clinical quality improvement lead. Draft a structured override-pattern review protocol for [name the specific AI tool and alert, e.g. 'the sepsis early-warning alert embedded in our EHR']. Include: a defined reporting period and override-rate calculation method; criteria for flagging an unusually high or low override rate; a sampling method for pulling individual override decisions for chart review; a structured rubric distinguishing an appropriate clinical exception from alert fatigue or automation bias; and an escalation pathway for confirmed findings. Write it as a document a quality committee could adopt directly, with numbered sections and no unexplained jargon. Then ask me clarifying questions about our tool's current alert volume so you can tighten the thresholds.","Act as a clinical quality improvement lead. I am attaching our existing alert-tuning logs or a prior override-rate report for [name the specific AI tool and alert, e.g. 'the sepsis early-warning alert embedded in our EHR'] so the protocol's thresholds are grounded in our actual baseline rather than invented: [paste or attach the existing logs or report in full]. Using that material, draft a structured override-pattern review protocol that includes: a defined reporting period and override-rate calculation method consistent with the attached baseline; criteria for flagging an unusually high or low override rate relative to that baseline; a sampling method for chart review; a rubric distinguishing an appropriate clinical exception from alert fatigue or automation bias; and an escalation pathway for confirmed findings. Write it as a document a quality committee could adopt directly.","Act as a clinical quality improvement lead. Draft a structured override-pattern review protocol for [name the specific AI tool and alert, e.g. 'the sepsis early-warning alert embedded in our EHR']. The protocol must include exactly these numbered sections, held in this order: (1) a defined reporting period and override-rate calculation method; (2) criteria for flagging an override rate as unusually high or unusually low relative to the tool's own baseline; (3) a sampling method for pulling a representative set of individual override decisions for chart review; (4) a structured interview or chart-review rubric that distinguishes an appropriate clinical exception from alert fatigue or automation bias; and (5) an escalation pathway specifying who receives a confirmed finding of a workflow or tool problem. Write it as a document a quality committee could adopt directly, with no unexplained jargon."]});
window.P.push({"n":24,"pt":"VIII","ct":"Prompting for Bias Mitigation and Fairness Review","t":"Assumption-Surfacing Check for a Clinical Recommendation","u":"Prompts a clinician to have an AI assistant explicitly state the demographic, epidemiological, or data-quality assumptions behind a clinical recommendation or differential before the clinician relies on it, and to flag where those assumptions may not fit the specific patient.","no":"Use ChatGPT because its GPT-5.x health-response tuning specifically targets asking clarifying questions before finalizing an answer and explaining uncertainty explicitly rather than asserting false confidence, exactly the behavior this prompt is designed to elicit. Claude becomes the better choice once the assumption check needs to be turned into a formally documented, long-form fairness-review memo per section 24.4. Switch to Gemini only if the assumption check needs to be run against an entire attached guideline or protocol document rather than a self-contained vignette.","v":["Before giving your final answer, work through this clinical question in two parts. First, state explicitly what demographic, epidemiological, or data-quality assumptions you are relying on: typical patient age or sex for this presentation, assumed prevalence in the population you are reasoning from, and whether any input value has known reliability differences across patient groups. Second, ask me one clarifying question if any of those assumptions might not fit my specific patient before you finalize your answer. Then give your recommendation, explicitly flagging any part of it that depends on an assumption I have not confirmed applies to this patient. Clinical question: [insert your case here].","I am pasting the full guideline or protocol document this clinical question is being reasoned against, so any demographic or epidemiological assumptions can be checked against the actual source rather than general recall: [paste the full guideline or protocol document here]. Before giving your final answer, work through this clinical question in two parts. First, state explicitly what demographic, epidemiological, or data-quality assumptions in the attached guideline you are relying on, and whether any input value has known reliability differences across patient groups per the attached text. Second, ask me one clarifying question if any assumption might not fit my specific patient. Then give your recommendation, flagging any part that depends on an unconfirmed assumption. Clinical question: [insert your case here].","Before giving your final answer, work through this clinical question and produce a formally documented fairness-review memo with exactly these labeled sections, held precisely: 'Assumptions Relied Upon' (demographic, epidemiological, and data-quality assumptions, including typical patient age or sex, assumed prevalence, and any input value with known reliability differences across patient groups), 'Assumptions Requiring Confirmation for This Patient,' and 'Recommendation, With Assumption-Dependent Portions Explicitly Flagged.' Do not blend flagged and unflagged content. If a needed confirmation is missing, note it rather than guessing. Clinical question: [insert your case here]."]});
window.P.push({"n":25,"pt":"IX","ct":"Clinical Validation of AI Tools Before Deployment","t":"Local Validation Protocol Outline","u":"Use this prompt when a governance committee needs a first-draft, structured validation protocol for a specific AI tool before it can be brought to a vote, turning a vendor's product description into a document that specifies thresholds, sample size, subgroup analysis, and a go/no-go structure the committee can revise rather than build from scratch.","no":"Use Claude because this document must hold a complex, multi-part structure (five required sections, each with sub-requirements) with disciplined adherence to formatting instructions across a document a governance committee, and potentially legal counsel, will rely on later. Claude's strength in long, structurally faithful written output, reinforced by the research brief's framing of it as the strongest choice for documents another clinician or reviewer will depend on, outperforms a more conversational drafting style here. ChatGPT is a reasonable second choice if the team wants an iterative, question-and-answer drafting session instead of a single structured output.","v":["Act as a clinical AI validation lead drafting a protocol for a hospital governance committee. The tool is [name the AI tool, its intended use, and the clinical setting]. Vendor-reported performance figures are: [paste vendor figures and cohort description]. First, ask me any clarifying questions about our target population or pilot timeline that would shape the protocol. Then draft a validation protocol with these sections, in order: primary and secondary performance thresholds to pre-register; minimum sample size and pilot duration given the target event's base rate; required subgroup analyses by age, race and ethnicity, language, and care setting; ground-truth adjudication and blinding approach; and a go, no-go, conditional-go decision structure. Use clear numbered sections, flag every missing number a stakeholder must supply, and do not invent statistics.","Act as a clinical AI validation lead drafting a protocol for a hospital governance committee. I am pasting the vendor's full validation study or technical report in its entirety below, rather than a summary, so the protocol is grounded in the actual reported methodology and cohort details: [paste the complete vendor validation study or report here]. The tool is [name the AI tool, its intended use, and the clinical setting]. Draft a validation protocol with these sections, in this order: 1) primary and secondary performance thresholds to pre-register, referencing the attached study's own reported figures; 2) minimum sample size and pilot duration given the target event's base rate as stated in the attachment; 3) required subgroup analyses; 4) ground-truth adjudication and blinding approach; 5) a go, no-go, conditional-go decision structure. Flag every missing number a stakeholder must supply, and do not invent statistics.","Act as a clinical AI validation lead drafting a protocol for a hospital governance committee. The tool is [name the AI tool, its intended use, and the clinical setting]. Vendor-reported performance figures are: [paste vendor figures and cohort description]. Draft a validation protocol with exactly these sections, held precisely in this order: 1) primary and secondary performance thresholds to pre-register before piloting; 2) minimum sample size and pilot duration needed given the target event's estimated base rate; 3) required subgroup analyses by age, race and ethnicity, language, and care setting; 4) ground-truth adjudication method and blinding approach; 5) a go, no-go, conditional-go decision structure. Use clear numbered sections, flag every place a real clinical stakeholder must supply a missing number, and do not invent statistics."]});
window.P.push({"n":26,"pt":"IX","ct":"Post-Deployment Monitoring and Model Drift","t":"Monitoring Dashboard Metric Definitions","u":"Use this prompt when a governance team needs to convert a validated tool's original performance thresholds and pilot data into precise, unambiguous KQI definitions for an ongoing monitoring dashboard, especially when the source material includes a long validation report or several months of raw override logs that need to be digested in full.","no":"Use Gemini because this task requires ingesting a full validation report plus months of raw operational logs in a single pass rather than a pre-summarized excerpt, and Gemini's large context window is a genuine, real differentiator for holding that much source material at once without chunking or losing detail. Claude is the stronger choice instead if the deliverable is a shorter, already-scoped set of definitions rather than one derived from large attached source documents; ChatGPT is a reasonable choice for an iterative, conversational version of this same exercise.","v":["Act as a clinical AI monitoring lead building dashboard metric definitions for a tool that's already narrowly scoped. For each of these four KQI categories, performance, workflow, equity, and safety, define one to two metrics with an exact numerator, denominator, measurement window, data source, and named owner role. Base initial alert thresholds on the performance figures I give you: [paste key performance figures here]. Flag any metric where the data I've given isn't enough to set a defensible threshold. Ask me clarifying questions about data source availability before finalizing, then output the result as a table with columns for KQI category, metric name, definition, threshold, and owner role.","Act as a clinical AI monitoring lead building dashboard metric definitions from a tool's original validation report and recent operational logs, both pasted or attached in full below, potentially spanning months of raw records: [paste or attach the full validation report, then the full operational logs here]. Read all of it before responding. For each of these four KQI categories, performance, workflow, equity, and safety, define one to two metrics with an exact numerator, denominator, measurement window, data source, and named owner role. Base the initial alert thresholds on the performance figures in the attached validation report rather than inventing new ones. Flag any metric where the attached data does not contain enough information to set a defensible threshold, and list what additional data would be needed. Output the result as a table with columns for KQI category, metric name, definition, threshold, and owner role.","Act as a clinical AI monitoring lead building dashboard metric definitions for an already-scoped monitoring initiative. For each of these four KQI categories, held in this exact order, performance, workflow, equity, and safety, define one to two metrics with an exact numerator, denominator, measurement window, data source, and named owner role. Base the initial alert thresholds on the performance figures I provide: [paste key performance figures here]. Flag any metric where the data given is insufficient to set a defensible threshold, and list what additional data would be needed. Output the result as a disciplined table with columns for KQI category, metric name, definition, threshold, and owner role, formatted so it can be handed directly to the dashboard-build team."]});
window.P.push({"n":27,"pt":"IX","ct":"Incident Reporting and Continuous Quality Improvement","t":"AI-Related Incident Report Narrative","u":"Use this prompt when a clinician or quality-improvement lead needs to convert the raw facts of an AI-related near-miss or safety event, gathered informally, into a clear, structured incident narrative suitable for a formal safety reporting system and for later root cause analysis, while flagging any missing detail before submission.","no":"Use ChatGPT because this task benefits from a genuinely conversational, clarifying-question-first drafting pattern before finalizing, which matches ChatGPT's demonstrated strength in health-response quality improvements that specifically emphasize asking clarifying questions before answering rather than asserting a complete narrative from incomplete facts. Claude is the stronger choice instead once the facts are already fully gathered and the task shifts to producing a single, long, formally structured document for a governance committee or legal review; Gemini is a reasonable choice if the raw facts include a long attached transcript or chart excerpt that needs to be read in full before drafting begins.","v":["Act as a patient safety quality-improvement specialist helping me write a structured AI-related incident report narrative. Here are the raw facts: [describe the event: the AI tool and version involved, what happened, when it was caught, by whom, and any patient impact]. Ask me clarifying questions first if any of the following is missing or unclear: whether the AI output was overridden or acted upon, whether the event was caught before or after reaching the patient, and the de-identified prompt and output text if available. Once you have enough detail, write the narrative in this order: event summary, timeline, AI tool details, immediate impact, and a preliminary note on which root cause branch, model failure, workflow failure, or human-factors failure, seems most consistent with the facts, flagged as preliminary and pending formal RCA. Remove any patient-identifiable detail.","Act as a patient safety quality-improvement specialist helping me write a structured AI-related incident report narrative. I am pasting or attaching the full transcript or chart excerpt surrounding this event, since I want you to read all of it before drafting anything: [paste or attach the full transcript or chart excerpt here]. Also here are the raw facts: [describe the event: the AI tool and version involved, what happened, when it was caught, by whom, and any patient impact]. Using the full attached material plus these facts, write the narrative in this order: event summary, timeline, AI tool details, immediate impact, and a preliminary note on which root cause branch, model failure, workflow failure, or human-factors failure, seems most consistent, flagged as preliminary and pending formal RCA. Remove any patient-identifiable detail from the narrative.","Act as a patient safety quality-improvement specialist producing a single, long, formally structured incident narrative for governance and legal review, using the fully gathered facts below: [paste the complete gathered facts: the AI tool and version involved, what happened, when it was caught, by whom, patient impact, whether the output was overridden, when the event was caught relative to the patient, and de-identified prompt and output text if available]. Write the narrative with exactly these labeled sections, held precisely in this order: 'Event Summary,' 'Timeline,' 'AI Tool Details,' 'Immediate Impact,' and 'Preliminary Root Cause Assessment' (model failure, workflow failure, or human-factors failure, explicitly flagged as preliminary and pending formal RCA). Remove any patient-identifiable detail from the narrative."]});
window.P.push({"n":28,"pt":"X","ct":"HIPAA, Data Governance, and De-Identification for AI Use","t":"Draft an AI Data Classification Policy","u":"Use this prompt when a governance committee needs a first-draft internal policy that classifies AI use cases by data sensitivity and maps each tier to required safeguards, as a starting point for legal and privacy review rather than a finished policy.","no":"Claude's strength in long-form structured writing with faithful adherence to formatting instructions suits a policy document meant to be reviewed, edited, and formally adopted. ChatGPT is a reasonable alternative if the committee wants to iterate conversationally on specific sections before finalizing. Gemini adds little here since the task does not involve a large source document or multimodal input.","v":["Act as a healthcare data governance consultant. Draft an internal AI data classification policy for a hospital system with three tiers: Tier 1, no patient data, safe for any AI tool; Tier 2, de-identified patient data, requires an approved de-identification method; Tier 3, identifiable PHI, requires a signed business associate agreement and documented scope approval. For each tier, specify examples of qualifying use cases, required pre-use checks, and who must approve exceptions. Use clear section headers and a summary table. Then ask me clarifying questions about our organization's actual approval structure before finalizing, and flag any assumption you're making that legal counsel should confirm.","Act as a healthcare data governance consultant. I am attaching our existing IT security policy or data governance document for context, so the new AI data classification tiers align with it rather than conflicting: [paste or attach the existing policy document in full here]. Draft an internal AI data classification policy for a hospital system with three tiers: Tier 1, no patient data, safe for any AI tool; Tier 2, de-identified patient data, requires an approved de-identification method; Tier 3, identifiable PHI, requires a signed business associate agreement and documented scope approval. For each tier, specify examples of qualifying use cases, required pre-use checks, and who must approve exceptions, cross-referenced against the attached policy where relevant. Use clear section headers and a summary table.","Act as a healthcare data governance consultant. Draft an internal AI data classification policy for a hospital system with exactly three tiers, held in this structure throughout: Tier 1, no patient data, safe for any AI tool; Tier 2, de-identified patient data, requires an approved de-identification method before use; Tier 3, identifiable PHI, requires a signed business associate agreement and documented scope approval. For each tier, specify examples of qualifying use cases, required pre-use checks, and who must approve exceptions. Use clear section headers and a summary table. Flag any point where you are making an assumption about the organization's structure that should be confirmed by legal counsel before adoption. Format this as the first document in an ongoing governance reference set."]});
window.P.push({"n":29,"pt":"X","ct":"Prompting Safely: What Never to Paste Into a Public AI Tool","t":"Build a Team-Ready Paste Safety Briefing","u":"Use this prompt to generate a short, plain-language internal briefing that trains clinical staff on the consumer-versus-enterprise distinction and the pre-prompt checklist, suitable for a huddle handout or onboarding module rather than a formal policy document.","no":"ChatGPT's strength in iterative, plain-language drafting and its broad familiarity as a workplace writing tool make it well suited to a short internal training handout meant to be revised quickly and distributed widely. Claude is a reasonable alternative if the briefing needs to be folded into a longer, more formally structured onboarding manual with strict formatting requirements. Gemini adds little advantage here since the task involves no large source document or multimodal content.","v":["Act as a clinical informatics educator writing a one-page staff briefing, no more than 400 words, on what is safe to type into AI tools at work. Explain, in plain language a busy clinician can read in under two minutes, the difference between our approved enterprise AI account and any free or personal AI app, why that difference matters for patient privacy, and a five-step checklist to run through before submitting any text that mentions a patient. End with three concrete example prompts: one clearly safe, one clearly unsafe, and one borderline case rewritten to be safe. Use short paragraphs, bold the five checklist steps, avoid legal jargon, and ask me whether this reads right for a huddle handout before we finalize it as a reusable Custom GPT for onboarding new staff.","Act as a clinical informatics educator writing a one-page staff briefing, no more than 400 words, on what is safe to type into AI tools at work. If I attach a screenshot of our approved enterprise AI login screen or interface, use it to make the \"which tool am I using\" distinction concrete for staff. Explain, in plain language a busy clinician can read in under two minutes, the difference between our approved enterprise AI account and any free or personal AI app, why that difference matters for patient privacy, and a five-step checklist before submitting any text that mentions a patient. End with three example prompts: one clearly safe, one clearly unsafe, one borderline case rewritten to be safe. Bold the five checklist steps and avoid legal jargon.","Act as a clinical informatics educator drafting a section for a longer, formally structured onboarding manual on what is safe to type into AI tools at work. Produce a one-page section, no more than 400 words, with exactly these labeled subsections, held precisely: 'Enterprise vs. Personal AI Accounts' (why the distinction matters for patient privacy), 'Five-Step Pre-Prompt Checklist' (bolded steps), and 'Example Prompts' (one clearly safe, one clearly unsafe, one borderline case rewritten to be safe). Write it so it can be inserted directly into the manual without reformatting, in plain language a busy clinician can read in under two minutes, and avoid legal jargon throughout."]});
window.P.push({"n":30,"pt":"X","ct":"Vendor Contracts, Data Use Agreements, and Security Review","t":"Generate a Vendor Security Review Question Set","u":"Use this prompt when preparing to evaluate a new AI vendor, to generate a structured, categorized list of due-diligence questions to send during procurement, before any patient data is shared.","no":"Claude's structured long-form output and faithful adherence to a specified format suit a due-diligence document that multiple departments, legal, security, and clinical leadership, will read and rely on as written. ChatGPT works well as a follow-up tool for drafting the vendor-facing cover email once the question set is finalized. Gemini is not the natural first choice here, since the task does not involve a large source document or multimodal input to process.","v":["Act as a healthcare IT security and privacy consultant. Generate a structured vendor due-diligence question set for evaluating a new AI vendor that will process patient data, organized into five categories: security and compliance artifacts, data use and training exclusions, data residency, breach notification and incident response, and exit and data portability terms. For each category, provide 4 to 6 specific questions phrased so answers can be verified against documentation rather than accepted as a general assurance. Present the output as a numbered checklist by category, suitable for an RFP. Then draft a short cover email I could send this checklist to the vendor with.","Act as a healthcare IT security and privacy consultant. I am attaching our existing generic IT vendor security questionnaire or a prior RFP security section, so the new AI-specific question set builds on it rather than duplicating it: [paste or attach the existing questionnaire or RFP section here]. Generate a structured vendor due-diligence question set for evaluating a new AI vendor that will process patient data, organized into five categories: security and compliance artifacts, data use and training exclusions, data residency, breach notification and incident response, and exit and data portability terms, adding only what the attached material does not already cover. For each category, provide 4 to 6 specific questions phrased so answers can be verified against documentation. Present the output as a numbered checklist by category, suitable for an RFP.","Act as a healthcare IT security and privacy consultant. Generate a structured vendor due-diligence question set for evaluating a new AI vendor that will process patient data, organized into exactly five categories, held precisely in this order: security and compliance artifacts, data use and training exclusions, data residency, breach notification and incident response, and exit and data portability terms. For each category, provide 4 to 6 specific questions a health system should send to the vendor in writing during procurement, phrased so the vendor's answers can be verified against documentation rather than accepted as a general assurance. Present the output as a numbered checklist organized by category, suitable for attaching to a request for proposal and for legal, security, and clinical stakeholders to rely on as written."]});
window.P.push({"n":31,"pt":"XI","ct":"AI in Surgery, Anesthesia, and Perioperative Planning","t":"Structured Perioperative Handoff Draft","u":"Use this prompt to convert raw case facts into a structured SBAR-format handoff note for a shift change or PACU transfer, before a clinician reviews and finalizes it for the record.","no":"Use Claude because a handoff note is read and relied on by another clinician and demands rigid structural adherence and conservative, well-hedged language over conversational flexibility; Claude's strength in disciplined long-form structured output, and its institutional healthcare tooling, are built for exactly this kind of document. ChatGPT is a reasonable alternative when the nurse wants to converse through missing details interactively before finalizing. Gemini is not the natural fit here, since this task works from discrete supplied facts rather than a large source document requiring long-context ingestion.","v":["You are assisting a perioperative nurse in drafting a shift handoff note for a shift change or PACU transfer. Use only the facts I provide below; do not infer, estimate, or add any clinical detail I have not stated. Structure the output as SBAR: Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation, using only the values I supply for each section. If anything needed for a complete handoff is missing, such as a pending order or a current vital sign, ask me directly rather than filling it in or guessing, then produce the finalized note once I've answered. Here are the case facts: [insert procedure, findings, complications, current vitals, pending orders].","You are assisting a perioperative nurse in drafting a shift handoff note. I am pasting the discrete case facts below along with the full intraoperative anesthesia record or OR log for additional context: [paste procedure, findings, complications, current vitals, pending orders, then paste the full anesthesia record or OR log]. Use only what is explicitly stated in either source; do not infer, estimate, or add any clinical detail not present. Structure the output strictly as SBAR: Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation, drawing Background details from the attached record where relevant. Flag explicitly, in brackets, any point where you would need more information rather than filling it in.","You are assisting a perioperative nurse in drafting a shift handoff note. Use only the facts I provide below; do not infer, estimate, or add any clinical detail I have not stated. Structure the output strictly as SBAR, held precisely in this exact order: Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation. Under Situation, state the patient identifier placeholder, procedure, and current location. Under Background, list relevant history and intraoperative course exactly as given. Under Assessment, state current status using only the values I supply. Under Recommendation, list only the follow-up items I specify. Flag explicitly, in brackets, any point where you would need more information rather than filling it in. Here are the case facts: [insert procedure, findings, complications, current vitals, pending orders]."]});
window.P.push({"n":32,"pt":"XI","ct":"AI in Mental and Behavioral Health","t":"Behavioral Health Screening Trend Summary","u":"Use this prompt when you need to turn a series of raw PHQ-9 or GAD-7 scores into a neutral, chart-ready trend summary for clinician review, without introducing any risk judgment or diagnosis the clinician has not made.","no":"Use ChatGPT here because the task benefits from its tendency to ask clarifying questions before finalizing an answer, useful when a clinician supplies an incomplete score list, and because GPT-5.x's health-response updates specifically target recognizing when more information or explicit uncertainty is warranted rather than asserting false confidence. Claude is a reasonable alternative when the summary needs to slot into a longer structured note with strict formatting requirements; Gemini adds little here, since no large source document or image is involved.","v":["You are helping a clinician summarize a patient's behavioral health screening results for the chart. I will give you a list of dates, the instrument used, and the total score for each administration. Write a short, purely descriptive summary of the trend over time: state whether scores increased, decreased, or stayed stable, and by how many points, using only the numbers I provide. Do not diagnose, do not suggest a risk level, and do not recommend a treatment change. If any individual item score, such as a suicide-screening item, is included, note only that it was scored as given and state clearly that clinical risk determination is the reviewing clinician's responsibility. Ask me for any dates or scores you are missing before finalizing. Here is the data: [insert dates, instrument, scores].","You are helping a clinician summarize a patient's behavioral health screening results for the chart. I am pasting the patient's full longitudinal screening history below, potentially spanning years of PHQ-9 or GAD-7 administrations across multiple instruments: [paste the complete list of dates, instrument used, and total score for every administration]. Write a purely descriptive summary of the trend over time across the entire history: state whether scores increased, decreased, or stayed stable overall and within any notable periods, and by how many points, using only the numbers provided. Do not diagnose, suggest a risk level, or recommend a treatment change. If any individual item score, such as a suicide-screening item, is included, note only that it was scored as given, and state that clinical risk determination is the reviewing clinician's responsibility.","You are helping a clinician summarize a patient's behavioral health screening results for insertion into a longer structured note. I will give you a list of dates, the instrument used, and the total score for each administration. Produce output with exactly these labeled sections, held precisely: 'Score Trend' (a purely descriptive statement of whether scores increased, decreased, or stayed stable, and by how many points, using only the numbers provided), and 'Clinical Responsibility Note' (a fixed statement that clinical risk determination belongs to the reviewing clinician). Do not diagnose, suggest a risk level, or recommend a treatment change. If an item score such as a suicide-screening item is included, note only that it was scored as given. Here is the data: [insert dates, instrument, scores]."]});
window.P.push({"n":33,"pt":"XI","ct":"Advanced Prompt Chains and Custom Assistants","t":"Draft Custom Assistant Configuration Instructions","u":"Use this prompt when standing up a new institutional custom GPT, Gem, or Claude Project for one recurring task, to produce ready-to-paste configuration instructions plus a knowledge-file checklist before you build the assistant on your chosen platform.","no":"Use Gemini when a full guideline or policy PDF is pasted in alongside the task description, since Gemini's native long-context and document handling can hold the entire source in view while drafting instructions that reference it faithfully, and it is also the natural fit when the resulting Gem will live inside a Google Workspace or Vertex AI deployment. Use ChatGPT instead when the target is specifically a Custom GPT and you want to finish configuring it conversationally inside GPT Builder's guided flow. Use Claude when the priority is a long, disciplined instructions document that a governance reviewer will scrutinize, particularly if the assistant will ultimately run as a Claude Project inside an institutional Claude for Healthcare deployment.","v":["Act as a clinical informatics lead drafting configuration instructions for a new institutional Custom GPT. The assistant's single recurring task is: [describe the task]. Using only what I have given you, write complete custom instructions, under 1500 characters, stating the assistant's scope, required output format, what it must never do (diagnose, finalize without human review, invent citations), and how it should request missing information. Then list a knowledge-file checklist for its knowledge base, flagging any document type that must never be uploaded due to identifiable patient information. Finally, walk me through configuring this step by step inside GPT Builder, and propose an initial version number and a one-line changelog entry.","Act as a clinical informatics lead drafting configuration instructions for a new institutional Gem. The assistant's single recurring task is: [describe the task]. I am pasting the full guideline or policy document it must follow: [paste the complete document text here]. Using the entire attached document, write complete custom instructions, under 1500 characters, stating the assistant's scope, required output format, what it must never do (diagnose, finalize without human review, invent citations), and how it should request missing information, referencing the attached guideline's actual language. Then list a knowledge-file checklist for its knowledge base, flagging any document type that must never be uploaded due to identifiable patient information, and note whether this Gem should deploy through Vertex AI or Google Workspace for our team.","Act as a clinical informatics lead drafting configuration instructions for a new institutional Claude Project. The assistant's single recurring task is: [describe the task]. If relevant, here is the full guideline or policy document it must follow, to be kept attached as reference material across every session: [paste full document text]. Using only what I have given you, write complete custom instructions stating the assistant's scope, required output format, what it must never do (diagnose, finalize without human review, invent citations), and how it should request missing information. Then produce a knowledge-file checklist for the Project's attached documents, flagging any document type that must never be uploaded due to identifiable patient information. Finally propose an initial version number and a one-line changelog entry, formatted for a governance reviewer to scrutinize."]});
window.P.push({"n":34,"pt":"XII","ct":"Selecting and Piloting a Clinical AI Tool","t":"Structured Tool Needs-Assessment Brief","u":"Use this prompt when a selection committee needs a single, well-organized needs-assessment brief for a candidate clinical AI tool, before any vendor demo, so every evaluator is scoring the same problem statement against the same criteria.","no":"Claude is the strongest fit because a needs-assessment brief is a long, structurally disciplined document that a committee, and later an auditor, will rely on verbatim, and Claude holds a complex multi-section formatting instruction faithfully across a long output. ChatGPT is a reasonable substitute when the committee wants a more conversational back-and-forth to refine the problem statement first, since its iterative, clarifying-question pattern suits early-stage drafting. Gemini is worth switching to when the committee wants to paste an entire existing vendor RFP response or policy document directly into the same session for the brief to reference, since its large context window handles a full source document without pre-summarizing it.","v":["You are a clinical informatics analyst supporting a hospital AI selection committee. I will describe a clinical or administrative problem and a candidate AI tool category. First, ask me clarifying questions to sharpen the problem statement and confirm the measurable outcome we actually care about. Then produce a structured needs-assessment brief with these sections, in order: Problem Statement; Current-State Workflow, three to five steps; Candidate Tool Category and how it addresses the problem; a Scoring Criteria table with columns for Criterion, Weight, and Guiding Question, covering clinical impact, workflow burden, feasibility, regulatory complexity, and cost; and Open Questions for the vendor. Use plain, direct language a non-technical board member can follow. Problem: [describe the problem]. Candidate tool category: [name the category].","You are a clinical informatics analyst supporting a hospital AI selection committee. I am pasting an existing vendor RFP response or relevant internal policy document directly into this session so the brief references real, specific material rather than generic assumptions: [paste the full RFP response or policy document here]. I will also describe a clinical or administrative problem and a candidate AI tool category. Produce a structured needs-assessment brief with these sections, in order: Problem Statement, framed around a measurable outcome; Current-State Workflow, three to five steps; Candidate Tool Category and how it addresses the problem, citing the attached material where relevant; a Scoring Criteria table (Criterion, Weight, Guiding Question) covering clinical impact, workflow burden, feasibility, regulatory complexity, and cost; and Open Questions for the vendor. Problem: [describe the problem]. Candidate tool category: [name the category].","You are a clinical informatics analyst supporting a hospital AI selection committee. I will describe a clinical or administrative problem and a candidate AI tool category. Produce a structured needs-assessment brief with exactly these sections, held precisely in this order: Problem Statement, one sentence framed around a measurable outcome rather than the tool; Current-State Workflow, three to five steps; Candidate Tool Category and how it addresses the problem; a Scoring Criteria table with columns for Criterion, Weight, and Guiding Question, covering clinical impact, workflow burden, feasibility, regulatory complexity, and cost; and Open Questions for the vendor. Use plain, direct language a non-technical board member can follow, since the committee and a later auditor will rely on this document verbatim. Problem: [describe the clinical or administrative problem]. Candidate tool category: [name the category]."]});
window.P.push({"n":35,"pt":"XII","ct":"Training Clinical Staff on Safe, Effective AI Use","t":"Team AI-Literacy Training Session Outline","u":"Use this prompt when a training lead needs a ready-to-teach, hour-long session outline introducing a specific clinical team to safe AI prompting practices, built around example prompts drawn from an internal or published prompt library.","no":"ChatGPT is the strongest fit here because building a training outline benefits from the conversational, clarifying-question pattern OpenAI has tuned into recent GPT-5.x updates, letting a facilitator refine the plan interactively rather than accepting a single first draft. Claude is the better switch once the same outline needs to become a long, formally structured facilitator guide bound to a fixed institutional template. Gemini is worth using instead when the facilitator wants to paste an entire existing onboarding or policy document into the same session so the new outline stays consistent with material already in use.","v":["Act as a clinical AI training facilitator. Build a 60-minute training session outline for [describe the team, for example a 12-person outpatient nursing team] who are new to using AI assistants for [describe the task, for example drafting patient education handouts]. Structure the outline as: a 5-minute framing of what the tool is and is not for; a 15-minute walkthrough of one example prompt, including its use case and a required verification step; a 20-minute hands-on exercise where participants run the prompt against a de-identified example from their own work; a 15-minute discussion of what to escalate and to whom; and a 5-minute wrap-up with a one-page takeaway. Ask me clarifying questions about the team's workflow before finalizing the outline, and tell me if this is worth saving as a reusable Custom GPT for future training leads.","Act as a clinical AI training facilitator. I am attaching our existing onboarding manual or AI-use policy document so the new session outline stays consistent with material already in use: [paste or attach the existing onboarding or policy document here]. Build a 60-minute training session outline for [describe the team] who are new to using AI assistants for [describe the task], structured as: a 5-minute framing of what the tool is and is not for, consistent with the attached policy; a 15-minute walkthrough of one example prompt with a required verification step; a 20-minute hands-on exercise with a de-identified example from their own work; a 15-minute discussion of what to escalate and to whom, per the attached policy; and a 5-minute wrap-up with a one-page takeaway.","Act as a clinical AI training facilitator producing a formal facilitator guide bound to our fixed institutional training template. Build a 60-minute session outline for [describe the team, for example a 12-person outpatient nursing team] who are new to using AI assistants for [describe the task, for example drafting patient education handouts], structured with exactly these timed, labeled sections, held precisely in this order: Framing (5 minutes), Example Prompt Walkthrough (15 minutes, including use case and required verification step), Hands-On Exercise (20 minutes, de-identified example from participants' own work), Escalation Discussion (15 minutes, what to escalate and to whom), and Wrap-Up (5 minutes, one-page takeaway). Write it so it can be filed as the permanent facilitator record for this session."]});
window.P.push({"n":36,"pt":"XII","ct":"The Next Five Years: A Roadmap for Continuous Adoption","t":"Quarterly AI Horizon Scan Briefing","u":"Use this prompt when an AI governance committee needs a single synthesized briefing pulling together several source documents, vendor release notes, a regulatory bulletin, internal pilot summaries, into one prioritized summary ahead of a quarterly meeting.","no":"Gemini is the strongest fit because this prompt depends on ingesting several full source documents in one pass rather than a pre-summarized excerpt, and Gemini's large context window is built for holding that much source material directly. ChatGPT works well when the committee instead wants a shorter, conversational first pass with follow-up questions before the documents are finalized. Claude is the better switch once the briefing itself needs to become the permanent, formally structured governance-committee record with disciplined section formatting that will be filed and referenced later.","v":["You are supporting a hospital AI governance committee's quarterly horizon scan. I will summarize the key points from several source documents: vendor release notes, a regulatory bulletin, and internal pilot summaries. First, ask me any clarifying questions about which deployed tools matter most this quarter. Then produce a briefing with four sections: New Capabilities Worth Committee Attention, ranked by relevance to our currently deployed tools; Regulatory or Compliance Signals, flagging anything affecting a deployed tool; Recommended Actions, each tied to a named owner and deadline; and Items to Log for Next Quarter. Keep it under two pages, and note where a claim is vendor-reported rather than independently verified.","You are supporting a hospital AI governance committee's quarterly horizon scan. I am providing several source documents in full: vendor release notes, a regulatory bulletin, and internal pilot summaries: [paste or attach all source documents here]. Read all of them in full before responding. Produce a briefing with four sections: New Capabilities Worth Committee Attention, ranked by relevance to our currently deployed tools; Regulatory or Compliance Signals, flagging anything affecting a deployed tool; Recommended Actions, each tied to a named owner and a deadline; and Items to Log for Next Quarter. Keep the entire briefing under two pages. Do not repeat vendor marketing language uncritically; note where a claim is vendor-reported rather than independently verified. Source documents: [paste or attach the documents here].","You are supporting a hospital AI governance committee's quarterly horizon scan, producing the permanent, formally structured record that will be filed and referenced in future quarters. I am providing several source documents: vendor release notes, a regulatory bulletin, and internal pilot summaries. Read all of them in full and produce a briefing with exactly these four labeled sections, held precisely in this order: 'New Capabilities Worth Committee Attention' (ranked by relevance to our currently deployed tools), 'Regulatory or Compliance Signals' (flagging anything affecting a deployed tool), 'Recommended Actions' (each tied to a named owner and a deadline), and 'Items to Log for Next Quarter.' Keep the entire briefing under two pages, and note where a claim is vendor-reported rather than independently verified. Source documents: [paste or attach the documents here]."]});
